From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: a733: dp: enable the transmitter output before training, not after

THE DEFECT
-----------

On this board link training does not reach clock recovery unless four registers
are already set, and `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s .atomic_enable sets them after training rather than
before -- they live in sun60i_dp_video_configure(), which it calls once
sun60i_dp_train() has already returned. The `train` debugfs verb never sets
them at all and documents itself as standalone. So a real modeset fails even
once the connector reports connected, and the verb that was supposed to be the
instrument cannot train on its own either.

The four:

  PAD_DP_LNK_CLK_CFG            0x05760000 = 1   sun60i_dp_video_gates()
  PAD_DP_VID_CLK_CFG            0x05760004 = 1   sun60i_dp_video_gates()
  DP_TRANSMITTER_OUTPUT_ENABLE  0x05740080 = 1   UM 15.4.7.14
  DP_INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE        0x05740094 = 1   UM 15.4.7.16

THE MEASUREMENT
----------------

On the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-22. The original negative and positive
runs came first; the staircase
below was taken afterwards, on one boot, and then reproduced from cold on a
second.

  nothing written                     fails -- four boots running, always "CR
                                      round 4: every lane at maximum swing and
                                      still no lock", sink DPCD 0x202-0x207 =
                                      00 00 80 00 33 33
  both eDP_REG_PAD gates only         FAILS
  0x0080 + 0x0094 only, gates shut    FAILS
  all four                            TRAINS -- "link trained: 2700 Mb/s over 4
                                      lanes, CR in 2 round(s), EQ in 1", sink
                                      77 77 01 01 11 11
  all four + the MSA block            no change, still trains
  all four + TCON_TV1 enabled         no change, still trains

⚠ THE FIRST WRITE-UP OF THIS DEFECT WAS TOO COARSE AND IS RETRACTED HERE. It
said the pixel path had to be programmed and running. It does not: on the cold
confirmation run SRC0_MVID read 0x00000000 and TCON_TV1's TV_GINT0 at
0x05731004 read 0x00000000 -- no video clock arriving at the core, the timing
generator off -- and the link trained. Neither a scanning TCON nor a pixel
clock nor the MSA block is required, and the earlier open question --
which of the writes training needs -- is settled by
the staircase.

⬜ WHY these four is still not explained, and the patch says so at the function.
The UM gives the rule without the mechanism: TRANSMITTER_OUTPUT_ENABLE is to
be set only once the link output is configured, so that the controller does
not disturb the PHY while it powers up (15.4.7.14), and
INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE starts the NO_VIDEO pattern while no video stream runs
(15.4.7.16).

⚠ `drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream` ALREADY DREW THE RIGHT CONCLUSION AND THE CALL ORDER DEFEATED IT. Its
comment above those two writes, unchanged in this patch, ends by saying that
the source enable puts the NO_VIDEO pattern on the link while no stream is
enabled, which is exactly the state to be in while training runs. Its
debugfs help says the same thing to the user -- write "configure" before
link training, "stream" after it (`drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream`). Both are correct. The only
thing missing was a driver that did it.

WHAT THIS CHANGES
------------------

One new function, sun60i_dp_train_output_enable(), doing exactly those four
writes and nothing else, called from sun60i_dp_train_once() between
sun60i_dp_link_configure() and sun60i_dp_train_configure_ends(). Both entry
points into training go through sun60i_dp_train(), so ONE insertion covers the
`train` debugfs verb and .atomic_enable, and nothing depends any longer on a
debugfs verb having been run first.

After link_configure and not before it, because that is what 15.4.7.14 asks for
(set it once the link output is configured) and it is also the arrangement
the 2026-08-20 four-lane run used: `link`, then `configure`, then `train` --
where the enables survived a second sun60i_dp_link_configure() inside the
training call. Per rung rather than once per call, because a rate change is a
PHY teardown and rebuild on this part.

⚠ .atomic_enable IS NOT REORDERED, and that is the point rather than an
omission. The MSA and the transfer unit stay AFTER training: the transfer unit
is computed from the rate and lane count the ladder settles on
(sun60i_dp_video_transfer_unit() reads dp->link_rate and dp->link_lanes), so
programming it before the ladder would program it for a rung that may never be
used. Only the four writes moved, and they read neither field.

⚠ AND THE CRTC WAS NEVER THE PROBLEM. On the modeset path TCON_TV1 is already
enabled when this bridge is: drm_atomic_helper_commit_modeset_enables() calls
drm_atomic_helper_commit_crtc_enable() -- which reaches
sun60i_kms_crtc_atomic_enable() (sun60i-kms.c:542), the display engine, then
sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_enable() (:567) -- before it calls
drm_atomic_helper_commit_encoder_bridge_enable(), which is what reaches
.atomic_enable. Those are the three adjacent calls of one function,
drm_atomic_helper.c:1761, :1763 and :1765, and this driver's
.atomic_commit_tail is the stock drm_atomic_helper_commit_tail_rpm()
(sun60i-kms.c:636). Recorded because the first reading of this defect proposed
reordering CRTC against bridge, and that would have been a change to the commit
sequence in answer to a fault that is four register writes inside one driver.

DIAGNOSTICS
------------

No line is deleted. One is added: a missing eDP_REG_PAD window is now refused
at training with its address and the consequence named, where before the two
gates were simply never written and the link failed at maximum swing with
nothing pointing at the reg property. `drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream`'s `video` verbs, its help text and
`drm-a733-dp-instrument-the-training-failure-paths`'s failure decoding are untouched, and `echo 0 > train` on a bare boot
remains runnable -- it is the instrument that produced the negative above, and
this tree does not delete diagnostics.

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES DIFFERENTLY
---------------------------------

Read in allwinner-bsp, branch cubie-aiot-v1.4.8.

| vendor | ours |
|---|---|
| gets the four for free and never states the rule. Its DP path trains at edp_main_link_setup() (sunxi_drm_edp.c:4501) and only then writes video format, timings and transfer unit (:4511, :4516, :4521) -- the same shape as ours -- because edp_hw_enable() (:4479) has already opened the gates and the enables on the way in. Nothing in that driver says training depends on them | the dependency is named at the function that has it, with the staircase that measured it, and it is asserted per training attempt rather than inherited from an enable path |
| has no way to train without a full modeset, so this fault cannot occur there -- and equally cannot be measured there | the `train` verb is standalone in fact and not only in its documentation, which is what made the staircase possible at all |
| one line and a return code for a failed link | the give-up reason, the round counters, the sink's own DPCD 0x202-0x207 (`drm-a733-dp-instrument-the-training-failure-paths`), and now a named refusal when the second reg window is absent |
| trains at one rate and one lane count with no fallback | rate then lane ladder (`drm-a733-dp-link-training`), and the four writes re-asserted on every rung of it |

AND IN MAINLINE
----------------

Worth stating because it goes the other way. Surveyed in 6.18.36, the normal
order is train first and program MSA afterwards -- dw-dp.c:1673 then :1679
(MSA at :1252), analogix_dp_core.c:844 then :857, cdns-mhdp8546-core.c:1939
then :1982, tegra/sor.c:2879 then :2897, tc358767.c:1597 then :1603,
i915 intel_ddi.c:2937 then :2975. This patch KEEPS that order; it is the four
enables that move, not the video programming.

⚠ But the enables themselves have no mainline precedent, and one driver does
the exact opposite: cdns-mhdp8546-core.c:1393 "Disable framer for link
training" turns its video framer OFF for the duration. A grep of the tree for
any statement that a DP source's output or stream must be enabled before its
own link training returns nothing. So this is an A733 property, it is stated at
the function that depends on it, and nobody should carry it elsewhere by
analogy.

WHAT IT OWES
-------------

  1. THE VERB, ON A COLD BOOT, and this is the whole claim:

       echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/aux_enable
       echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/train
       cat   /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/train

     Nothing else -- no bring-up script, no TCON verb, no `video configure`.
     PASS: dmesg "link trained: 2700 Mb/s over 4 lanes", and the readback shows
     the SINK's bytes, DPCD 0x202-0x205 = 77 77 01 01, clock recovery done on
     every active lane, equalisation done, symbol locked, interlane aligned.
     FALSIFIED BY the signature of the failing run above -- "CR round 4: every lane at
     maximum swing and still no lock" and 00 00 80 00 33 33 -- which would mean
     the four writes are not the precondition after all, or are being defeated
     by their position after sun60i_dp_link_configure().

  2. THE MODESET, on a kernel carrying `drm-a733-kms-drm-device-for-the-display-pipe` and `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-tcon-dp-edge`:

       modetest -M sun60i-a733-kms -s <connector>@<crtc>:1920x1200

     PASS: "link trained: 2700 Mb/s over 4 lanes" followed by "video stream
     enabled ... the sink reports it in sync (DPCD 0x205)", and a picture.
     Falsified by "link training failed" on that path, or by a trained link
     with SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS non-zero, which would say the MSA and the
     transfer unit are being written for a different link than the one that
     trained.

  3. THE NEGATIVE THAT MAKES 1 MEAN SOMETHING, on the same boot and before it:
     with this patch reverted, the same two commands must still fail. Both arms
     on one kernel is the only admissible
     shape, and the alternative -- comparing against a different boot -- is how
     half a session was lost on 2026-08-22.

Checked only by an automated build -- NOT hardware-proven and not hardware-exercised in this form. What
has run on silicon is the register state, set by hand; no build of this driver
has yet made those writes itself.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
@@ -1955,6 +1955,69 @@ static int sun60i_dp_train_configure_ends(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * FOUR WRITES THE LINK WILL NOT TRAIN WITHOUT, AND THEY ARE NOT VIDEO.
+ *
+ * The two eDP_REG_PAD clock gates, TRANSMITTER_OUTPUT_ENABLE and
+ * INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE. Nothing here describes a mode, so nothing here depends
+ * on anything training decides -- which is what lets it run BEFORE the pattern
+ * while the MSA and the transfer unit stay after it, where they must be: the
+ * transfer unit is computed from the rate and the lane count the ladder settles
+ * on (sun60i_dp_video_transfer_unit()).
+ *
+ * THE MEASUREMENT, the 8 GiB board / 2026-08-22, staircase on one boot and
+ * reproduced from cold on a second, preceded by an original negative and
+ * positive run:
+ *
+ *   nothing written                          fails, four boots running, always
+ *                                            "every lane at maximum swing and
+ *                                            still no lock", sink DPCD
+ *                                            0x202-0x207 = 00 00 80 00 33 33
+ *   both eDP_REG_PAD gates only              FAILS
+ *   0x0080 + 0x0094 only, gates shut         FAILS
+ *   all four                                 TRAINS -- "2700 Mb/s over 4 lanes,
+ *                                            CR in 2 round(s), EQ in 1", sink
+ *                                            77 77 01 01 11 11
+ *   all four + the MSA block                 no change, still trains
+ *   all four + TCON_TV1 enabled              no change, still trains
+ *
+ * ⚠ AND THE PIXEL PATH IS NOT PART OF IT. On the cold confirmation run
+ * SRC0_MVID read 0x00000000 and TCON_TV1's TV_GINT0 at 0x05731004 read
+ * 0x00000000 -- no video clock at the core, the timing generator off -- and the
+ * link trained anyway. So the coarse reading this defect was first written up
+ * with, that "the pixel path must be programmed and running", is WRONG and is
+ * not what this function implements. Neither a scanning TCON, nor a pixel
+ * clock, nor the MSA block is required. Nothing here reaches outside this
+ * driver's own two register windows, and nothing here needs another device.
+ *
+ * ⬜ WHY these four is not explained. The UM gives the shape of the rule and
+ * not the reason: TRANSMITTER_OUTPUT_ENABLE is to be set only once the link
+ * output is configured, so that the controller does not disturb the PHY
+ * while it powers up (15.4.7.14) -- hence its position here,
+ * after sun60i_dp_link_configure() -- and INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE starts the
+ * NO_VIDEO pattern while no video stream runs
+ * (15.4.7.16). `drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream` wrote both of those facts down, and drew the right
+ * conclusion in as many words: NO_VIDEO is "exactly the state to be in while
+ * training runs". The call order defeated it. This is that conclusion made
+ * true on both paths into training rather than only on the one a user
+ * drives by hand.
+ */
+static int sun60i_dp_train_output_enable(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
+{
+	if (!dp->pad) {
+		dev_err(dp->dev,
+			"no eDP_REG_PAD window: this node needs a second reg entry at 0x05760000, and without its two clock gates this link does not reach clock recovery\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	sun60i_dp_video_gates(dp, true);
+
+	writel(TX_OUTPUT_ENABLE, dp->base + DP_TRANSMITTER_OUTPUT_ENABLE);
+	writel(INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE_SST, dp->base + DP_INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 /*
  * One attempt at one rate and one lane count: configure the PHY, tell both ends
  * the same thing, then CR and EQ.
@@ -1973,6 +2036,17 @@ static int sun60i_dp_train_once(struct sun60i_dp *dp, unsigned int rate_mbps,
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	/* Then the four writes without which this link does not reach clock
+	 * recovery on this board -- the measurement is at
+	 * sun60i_dp_train_output_enable(). Here rather than once per
+	 * sun60i_dp_train() call because a rate change is a PHY teardown and
+	 * rebuild, so every rung re-asserts them; they cost two register writes
+	 * and two gate writes and depend on nothing this loop produces.
+	 */
+	ret = sun60i_dp_train_output_enable(dp);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	ret = sun60i_dp_train_configure_ends(dp);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
@@ -2443,6 +2517,16 @@ static void sun60i_dp_bridge_atomic_enable(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 	/* Auto on both: the rate ladder asks the sink and the PHY, and the lane
 	 * ladder is the fallback DP 1.4 section 3.5.1.2.1 requires. A modeset must not
 	 * hardcode either -- that is the vendor behaviour `drm-a733-dp-link-training` replaced.
+	 *
+	 * ⚠ TRAINING BEFORE VIDEO IS CORRECT HERE, and it is correct for one
+	 * reason only: the four writes this board will not train without are
+	 * inside the call, at sun60i_dp_train_output_enable(). Everything
+	 * sun60i_dp_video_configure() adds on top of them -- the MSA and the
+	 * transfer unit -- was measured to make no difference to training, and
+	 * the transfer unit MUST stay on this side of it, because it is computed
+	 * from the rate and lane count the ladder settles on. Moving the video
+	 * configure above the training call would program it for a rung that may
+	 * never be used.
 	 */
 	ret = sun60i_dp_train(dp, 0, 0);
 	if (ret) {
